Let $\mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$. Given a finite set $F=\{\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_k\}$ of affine functions $\alpha_i:\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ of the form $\alpha_i(x)=m_i x+a_i$ with integers $m_i\ge 2$ and $a_i\ge 0$, and given a nonnegative integer $z$, let $E[F:z]\subseteq\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}$ denote the smallest set containing $z$ and closed under each $\alpha_i\in F$. The (upper) asymptotic density of a set $S\subseteq\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}$ is $\limsup_{N\to\infty}\frac{|S\cap[1,N]|}{N}$.

For each of the following six choices of $F=\{\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3\}$, prove or disprove that the asymptotic density of $E[F:0]$ equals $0$:

(1) $\alpha_1(x)=2x,\ \alpha_2(x)=3x+3,\ \alpha_3(x)=6x+10$;
(2) $\alpha_1(x)=2x,\ \alpha_2(x)=6x+3,\ \alpha_3(x)=3x+2$;
(3) $\alpha_1(x)=3x,\ \alpha_2(x)=2x+2,\ \alpha_3(x)=6x+15$;
(4) $\alpha_1(x)=3x,\ \alpha_2(x)=6x+2,\ \alpha_3(x)=2x+1$;
(5) $\alpha_1(x)=6x,\ \alpha_2(x)=3x+1,\ \alpha_3(x)=2x+2$;
(6) $\alpha_1(x)=6x,\ \alpha_2(x)=2x+1,\ \alpha_3(x)=3x+6$.

